---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Row from '../components/Row.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import NoteRef from '../components/NoteRef.astro';
import PostList from '../components/PostList.astro';
import ChartAgents from '../components/figures/ChartAgents.astro';
import EnergyAxis from '../components/figures/EnergyAxis.astro';
import ManyCharts from '../components/figures/ManyCharts.astro';
import ParetoFront from '../components/figures/ParetoFront.astro';
import LifeGraph from '../components/figures/LifeGraph.astro';
import NetGlyph from '../components/figures/NetGlyph.astro';
import RiverGauge from '../components/figures/RiverGauge.astro';
import Portrait from '../assets/profile.jpg';
import {
  EMAIL,
  GITHUB_URL,
  LINKEDIN_URL,
  SITE_DESCRIPTION,
  SITE_TITLE,
} from '../consts';
import { sitePath } from '../lib/paths';

const RIVER_URL = 'https://river.edjex.dev';
const TDMPC2_URL = 'https://github.com/Jex-y/tdmpc2-jax';
const thesis = sitePath('/blog/final-year-project/');
const cisco = sitePath('/blog/cisco-internship/');
---

<BaseLayout title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}>
  <main class="page">
    <Hero />

    <Row>
      <p>
        I'm a software engineer at Solve Intelligence, where I build AI tools
        for patent attorneys.<NoteRef n={1} />
      </p>
      <p>
        I studied computer science at Durham and finished top of my year.<NoteRef
          n={2}
        /> I also learned to row there, and spent a year running Trevelyan College
        Boat Club. Not wanting to wake up for an outing just to be canceled, I built
        a model that forecasts conditions on the Wear.<NoteRef n={3} />
      </p>
      <p>
        For my master's project I searched for tiny vision models that watch
        water fleas for signs of stress, an early warning of water pollution.<NoteRef
          n={4}
        />
      </p>

      <Fragment slot="left">
        <figure class="portrait">
          <Image
            src={Portrait}
            alt="Ed Jex at graduation in Durham, July 2025"
            width={360}
            height={450}
            fit="cover"
          />
          <figcaption>Durham, 2025.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <Note n={1}>
          Mostly our charts product, and the infrastructure that keeps agents
          running in the background at scale. More under Work.
          <svg
            slot="figure"
            viewBox="0 0 72 30"
            width="72"
            height="30"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <g stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.9">
              <line x1="36" y1="15" x2="8" y2="5"></line>
              <line x1="36" y1="15" x2="8" y2="25"></line>
              <line x1="36" y1="15" x2="64" y2="15"></line>
            </g>
            <circle cx="36" cy="15" r="3.6" fill="#000000"></circle>
            <circle cx="8" cy="5" r="2.6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#000000"
            ></circle>
            <circle cx="8" cy="25" r="2.6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#000000"
            ></circle>
            <circle cx="64" cy="15" r="2.6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#000000"
            ></circle>
          </svg>
        </Note>
        <Note n={3}>
          <a href={RIVER_URL}>river.edjex.dev</a>
        </Note>
      </Fragment>

      <Fragment slot="right">
        <Note n={2}>
          MEng, first class, with the prizes for the top project and the top
          overall grade.
        </Note>
        <Note n={4}>
          2.2 µJ a frame on a Raspberry Pi with a Hailo-8L. More under Research.
          <NetGlyph
            slot="figure"
            width={72}
            height={36}
            radius={2.4}
            layers={[
              { x: 8, ys: [6, 14, 22, 30] },
              { x: 36, ys: [6, 14, 22, 30] },
              { x: 64, ys: [12, 24] },
            ]}
          />
        </Note>
        <LifeGraph />
      </Fragment>
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="work">Work</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        I joined Solve straight out of uni. Most of my work is on Charts:
        spreadsheets for IP search and analysis, each filled in by an agent that
        plans the work and delegates sections of it to subagents.<NoteRef
          n={5}
        />
        Work that used to go out to a search specialist, or take tens of hours of
        an attorney's time, comes back in minutes. I took it from a prototype to
        a production system that thousands of customers use every day, and they now
        use it for all sorts of tables it was never designed for.
      </p>
      <ChartAgents />
      <p>
        I also built the infrastructure that runs thousands of agent traces a
        day, which now underpins most of the platform. Customers can leave
        agents working in the background, close the tab, and come back to them
        still streaming live.
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={5}>
        Invalidity, freedom to operate, infringement, standard-essential patents
        and portfolio reviews. The rows and columns are whatever the analysis
        needs, such as claim elements against prior art or patents against
        products, and every cell is cited. In production since January 2026.
      </Note>
    </Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        Three problems took most of the time. The first was streaming. Agent
        messages and chart edits both have to reach the browser instantly,
        exactly once, and survive a bad connection. Every change is an event:
        the ones worth keeping are written to Postgres, the rest are applied and
        passed on,<NoteRef n={6} /> and all of them travel over Redis to the API,
        then to the browser over server-sent events, or long polling when the network
        is poor. A client that drops reconnects from a cursor into what's already
        persisted, so it catches up with no gaps and no duplicates.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second was time to first token. We grew from working on one document
        at a time to large projects, and were loading and parsing documents,
        sometimes more than once, for agents that never read them. Now they load
        lazily, and OCR splits each document into page ranges that run in
        parallel and are stitched back together.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third was scale. Agent volume has grown exponentially, and traces
        have gone from short, bounded tasks to long, open-ended ones. Fitting
        many agents on one container meant hunting down work that held the GIL
        and stalled every other agent beside it.
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={6}>
        An agent's messages are kept. A change to a document's state is applied
        on the backend, and the browser only needs to show the result.
      </Note>
      <ManyCharts slot="right" />
    </Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        Before Solve I spent a summer at Cisco, working on the Webex web client.<NoteRef
          n={7}
        />
      </p>
      <Note slot="right" n={7}>
        Twelve weeks, summer 2022. <a href={cisco}>This is what I worked on</a>.
      </Note>
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="research">Research</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        My thesis asked how little energy a useful vision model can spend, and
        what it has to give up to get there. The task was watching water fleas,
        which spin when the water around them is polluted, on a device powered
        by a microbial fuel cell. Treating detection as finding points rather
        than drawing boxes, then searching five hundred architectures and
        measuring each one on the hardware, gave a three-layer network that uses
        2.2 µJ a frame: 680 times less than YOLO11-nano.<NoteRef n={8} />
      </p>
      <p>
        The same approach works well beyond water fleas: anywhere you don't need
        the general reasoning of a large model, but do need low latency, low
        cost or high throughput.
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={8}>
        Supervised by Dr Farshad Arvin. <a href={thesis}>The full write-up</a>.
      </Note>
      <Fragment slot="right">
        <EnergyAxis>
          Energy per frame in µJ, measured on the device. Log scale.
        </EnergyAxis>
        <ParetoFront>
          The 22 models on the Pareto front, out of five hundred measured on the
          device.
        </ParetoFront>
      </Fragment>
    </Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        On the side I rewrote TD-MPC2, a model-based reinforcement learning
        method, in JAX. Compiling the planning and update steps makes it train
        five to ten times faster than the original.<NoteRef n={9} /> For two years
        I also ran undergraduate labs, in Computational Thinking and in Networks
        and Systems.
      </p>
      <Note slot="right" n={9}>
        JAX, Flax and MuJoCo. <a href={TDMPC2_URL}
          >github.com/Jex-y/tdmpc2-jax</a
        >
      </Note>
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="river">On the river</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p>
        I learned to row at Durham, and in 2023 I became president of Trevelyan
        College Boat Club: a year of training plans, races, budgets and boats.
        Whether we could go out at all depended on the Wear, so in 2024 I built
        a deep-learning forecast of the river level and a dashboard the clubs
        check before they boat.<NoteRef n={10} />
      </p>
      <Note slot="left" n={10}>
        PyTorch for the model, Rust and ONNX to serve it. <a href={RIVER_URL}
          >river.edjex.dev</a
        >
      </Note>
      <RiverGauge slot="right" />
    </Row>

    <Row><h2 id="writing">Writing</h2></Row>

    <PostList />

    <Row><h2 id="cv">Elsewhere</h2></Row>

    <Row>
      <p class="elsewhere">
        Write to {EMAIL}. Code is on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>, the rest
        is on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>, and there's a <a
          href={sitePath('/cv.pdf')}>CV</a
        > if you want one.
      </p>
    </Row>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .portrait {
    gap: 6px;
  }

  .portrait img {
    display: block;
    width: 160px;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .elsewhere a {
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1180px) {
    .portrait img {
      width: 180px;
    }
  }
</style>
